From c30745052975f28fc5db44c37b54c58cc7a4dcf1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 04:58:02 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades] Without a whitelist, a mode at the video's rate at
 another size of the display

Without a whitelist of the user, Kodi looks at the modes of the display that are at least the size of
the mode it is in, and takes one at the frame rate of a video (or twice it, or 2.5 times it) only at the
size of the video or at the width of the menus. An LG C5 had its menus at
2560x1440, where it has no mode of 23.976 Hz: a film of 1920x1080 played at the 119.998 Hz of the
menus, one picture held a refresh longer every 0.2 s, though the TV has 3840x2160 and 1920x1080 at
23.976 Hz.

Now, after Kodi's own tiers of the exact rate and of twice it, and before 3:2 pulldown at the width of
the menus, a mode at the frame rate (then at twice it) is looked for at any size of the display that is
not smaller than the video (ExactRateElsewhere.h, CResolutionUtils::FindExactRateElsewhere()): the size
of the display's preferred mode first (Kodi's desktop resolution), then the nearest size at or above
that of the menus, then the largest under them. A video not known to be progressive takes no mode of 30
Hz or less but 24 Hz, as Kodi's default whitelist does not.

Only without a whitelist of the user, which decides as it did, and only for SDR video in 2D: native
Dolby Vision keeps its transport mode and its cadence (FindDVTransportResolution() and the rule of
cadence are not touched), and HDR10, HDR10+, HLG and a video whose kind is not known keep Kodi's choice
as it was. A display without a mode at that rate at any size (the Samsung Odyssey G60SD) is as before.
kodi.log says what was found and why.

Checked: a host test (exact_rate_test.cpp: the LG C5 with the menus at 1440p gets 3840x2160 at 23.976
Hz; the G60SD nothing; the user's whitelist, Dolby Vision, HDR, 3D nothing; the order of the sizes;
twice the rate after the rate; a video not known to be progressive) with its mutants; the changed files
and the sources that include them compile with the commands of the image's build. Not run on a display.
